  "content": {
    "id": "p51-q49-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "49",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p51-q49-stem",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Proof by contradiction",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ],
              "colour": "#8b5cc7"
            },
            {
              "type": "break"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Consider the equation ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\sin(2x)+\\sin(3x)=2",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p51-q49-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why $\\sin(2x)=1$ and $\\sin(3x)=1$ simultaneously.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Each sine is at most 1, so their sum can equal 2 only when both equal 1.",
          "worked": "Since $\\sin(2x)\\le1$ and $\\sin(3x)\\le1$, if either were less than 1 their sum would be less than 2. Therefore both equal 1 simultaneously.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q49-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the solution of $\\sin(2x)=1$, across any domain of $x$, can be expressed as\n\n$$\\frac{\\pi}{4}+m\\pi,$$\n\nwhere $m$ is an integer.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+m\\pi$, $m\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2x&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+2m\\pi\\\\x&=\\frac{\\pi}{4}+m\\pi\\end{align*}$$\n\nHere $m\\in\\mathbb Z$, because sine equals 1 at $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and repeats every $2\\pi$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q49-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that similarly, any solution of $\\sin(3x)=1$ must be in the form $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\frac{2n\\pi}{3}$, where $n$ is an integer.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}+\\dfrac{2n\\pi}{3}$, $n\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}3x&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+2n\\pi\\\\x&=\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\frac{2n\\pi}{3}\\end{align*}$$\n\nHere $n\\in\\mathbb Z$, using the same $2\\pi$ period.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q49-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Deduce that $\\sin(2x)+\\sin(3x)=2$ has no solutions.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No solutions.\n\nEquating the general solutions gives $12m-8n=-1$, impossible because the left side is even.",
          "worked": "Suppose a solution exists. The two sine terms sum to 2, so by part (a), both must equal 1. Therefore the expressions from parts (b) and (c) must agree for integers $m,n$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{\\pi}{4}+m\\pi&=\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\frac{2n\\pi}{3}\\\\3+12m&=2+8n\\\\12m-8n&=-1\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe left side is even, whereas $-1$ is odd. This contradiction proves that there are no solutions.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      }
    ]
  },
